Simple solution: make all the orcs popes. }^P

Seriously, though, orc had to be changed to =F6rkki due to the Law of
Hobson-Jobson. It's the same reason Algonquian pawcohiccora became
pokahickory, pohickory, and finally just hickory, and Siraj-ud-Dawla
became Sir Roger Dowler. It is becoming somewhat fashionable to keep
words in their foreign form to show how cosmopolitan we are, but it is
still quite common and normal to respell words or even change them
significantly when borrowing them from another language, both to fit
the orthography of the language they're entering, and to fit the mouths
of its speakers.

I don't think fire elemental is a particularly hard case, however. You
could translate it as "living fire" and get the point across nicely.
You lose the connection to the four elements, but the fact that "living
water", "living earth", and "living wind" are also wandering around
beating on you would get that point across, too, and none of them
should be very hard to write in Mandarin, I expect.

Some of the others, especially D&D ones like gelatinous cubes,
piercers, umber hulks, shriekers, and black puddings would be harder to
convey.

Proper nouns like Mjollnir you can transliterate as best you can, and
the players can always google them if they aren't familiar with them,
but would there be Chinese myths that would seem glaring omissions if
they aren't added? Eastern dragons; Fenghuang, the Chinese phoenix;
Chi-Lin, the Chinese unicorn; T'ao T'ieh; perhaps A Bao A Qu, if Borges
was correct.

I agree with the previous posters, though, that the idioms and puns
would be the absolute nightmare, though.
